ProsperoSunshine: don't crash when the control port can't be bound

net::host_create read host->socket right after enet_host_create, which returns nullptr when the UDP socket can't be
created or bound, for example when another process holds the control port. The RTSP thread then crashed with a null
read during ANNOUNCE, which took the whole host down (GitHub issue 24, firmware 11.40). Now host_create logs the port
and errno and returns the empty host, so control_server_t::bind fails, start_broadcast logs "Couldn't bind Control
server" and the session ends while the host keeps running.

--- a/src/network.cpp
+++ b/src/network.cpp
@@ -4,6 +4,8 @@
  */
 // standard includes
 #include <algorithm>
+#include <cerrno>
+#include <cstring>
 #include <sstream>
 
 // local includes
@@ -218,6 +220,13 @@
 
     // Maximum of 128 clients, which should be enough for anyone
     auto host = host_t {enet_host_create(af == IPV4 ? AF_INET : AF_INET6, &addr, 128, 0, 0, 0)};
+    if (!host) {
+      // ENet returns nullptr when the socket can't be created or bound (another process holds the port). The caller
+      // reports that and ends the session; reading host->socket here would crash the host.
+      const int bind_errno = errno;
+      BOOST_LOG(error) << "ENet couldn't open UDP port "sv << port << ": "sv << std::strerror(bind_errno) << " (errno "sv << bind_errno << ')';
+      return host;
+    }
 
     // Enable opportunistic QoS tagging (automatically disables if the network appears to drop tagged packets)
     enet_socket_set_option(host->socket, ENET_SOCKOPT_QOS, 1);
